From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 06:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Dock: shelf-2's icon tiles: 40 px, the plate filling the
 tile, squircle and lit

The dock's tile was 45 px with an 11 px gap and its icons drew their whole
canvas, so a plate was smaller than the tile. The tile is shelf-2's 40 px
with an 8 px gap; an app icon is drawn through syncAppIconPlate with its
icon bin as the masked clip, cut as shelf-2's squircle (30%, CSS
corner-shape: squircle) and lit as its data-icon="lit" (a top sheen and a
bottom shade, a 1 px white top rim, a 0.5 px white ring and a 1 px dark
bottom rim, each following the corner). An icon without a plate sits on the
dock's own gradient plate. The show-apps glyph keeps its mask.

For the dock's tiles only (syncAppIconPlate's dock argument), the plate
helper follows group transforms: Prairie r42 draws the r40 art 1.12 times
about the centre, so its plate is 963.2 of 1024, not the 860 rect alone,
and a plated icon keeps the tile's size in layout (a fixed size), so a clip
that sizes to its child stays the tile; St draws the larger texture into
that box and the plate's scale shows it. Banners, offers and the overlay
indicator read and draw icons as before.

LumaIconMask gains squircle and lit uniforms, off unless an effect's
squircle or lit field is set (the dock's). Every mask sets both on each
paint: the GL program is shared by all of them, so a uniform one left unset
would keep another icon's value.
